```

Each single change is safe: every majority of the old set meets every majority of the new one, so the two can't decide different things. Two changes that both start from C are not: the last line of the first run is exactly the 2015 bug, S1 elected by {S1, S4, S5} under D while E's entry was committed on {S2, S3}. The rule that a new leader commits its no-op first stops the second change from starting until the first one is settled, so the sets always differ by one server. The second run is the paper's three-to-five jump: two old servers and three new ones can each form a majority.

